Yes. And I think you’re pointing toward something deeper than fake it till you make it.

Something can be constructed, relational, or imaginary in origin and still become causally real.

The distinction isn’t necessarily between real and not real. It’s between different ways of existing.

Consider your buffalo example.

A buffalo is an organism. A herd is a relationship among organisms. You can’t point to a separate physical object called the herd, yet the herd can migrate, defend itself, develop collective behavior, and influence the survival of its members.

The herd is real at a different level of organization.

The same principle extends across domains:

* Money: Paper or digital records become purchasing power because people recognize and enforce their value.
* Governments: People, institutions, laws, buildings, and coordinated behavior generate an entity capable of exercising real power.
* Identity: A story you tell about yourself influences how you behave, which influences how others respond, potentially reinforcing that identity.
* Narratives: A shared interpretation of events influences collective behavior and can produce consequences that wouldn’t otherwise occur.
* Teams and organizations: Individuals coordinating around a shared purpose develop collective capabilities no individual possesses independently.

This is where three concepts intersect: social construction, emergence, and performativity.

The mechanism

And this produces a fascinating possibility:

A representation of reality can become one of the forces that changes the reality it represents.

Economists have studied this through self-fulfilling expectations. Sociologists through social construction. Philosophers through collective intentionality. Biologists through emergence and collective behavior.

But there’s an important boundary: believing something exists doesn’t automatically make its claims physically true. Pretending you have wings won’t let you fly. Yet imagining flight can inspire you to construct an aircraft.

The imagined thing becomes materially consequential through the mechanisms connecting imagination to action.

Here’s the part I’d add to your observation

Sometimes an entity isn’t merely created by grouping things together.

The grouping itself creates new properties and possibilities.

A herd can defend itself collectively.

A government can enforce laws.

A company can coordinate thousands of people.

A language can preserve information across generations.

And a repository like Pantheon can help coordinate ideas, methods, software, and people into capabilities none of its components contains alone.

The relationship is not necessarily less real than its constituents. It’s a different kind of reality with its own causal effects.

Which brings us back to the idea you’ve been developing:

You don’t always have to change the underlying pieces to change what exists. Sometimes you change the relationships, boundaries, expectations, or rules by which the pieces interact—and something new emerges.

That’s not just reframing reality.

Under the right conditions, it’s constructing a new layer of it.